update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Magoodhoo Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 12 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 5 hours from Male)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Magoodhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Magoodhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Magoodhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Magoodhoo stands out for its marine research station and unique blend of traditional island life with scientific study. Unlike other islands, Magoodhoo offers opportunities to learn about marine conservation while experiencing authentic Maldivian culture."},{"question":"Can visitors participate in research activities on Magoodhoo?","answer":"While formal research participation is typically limited to scientists and students, visitors may be able to join educational programs or tours of research facilities when available. Check with your guesthouse or the research station for current opportunities."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Magoodhoo?","answer":"Magoodhoo''s waters offer excellent marine biodiversity, including healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. Common sightings include reef sharks, various ray species, abundant reef fish, and sea turtles. The house reef provides easy access to this underwater world directly from shore."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'magoodhoo-faafu';

-- magoodhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Noonu Atoll, Northern Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 1,100 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"1.1 km long, 0.7 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"200 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Seaplane or domestic flight + speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Pristine beauty, magnificent beaches, excellent snorkeling"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Nature enthusiasts, photographers, snorkelers, cultural travelers"}],"island_overview":["Magoodhoo Island is a pristine local island in Noonu Atoll that offers visitors an unspoiled glimpse into authentic Maldivian life. This magnificent island combines stunning natural beauty with an untouched atmosphere, providing an ideal destination for travelers seeking genuine cultural experiences and pristine natural splendor.","Known for its beautiful beaches, crystal-clear lagoons, and excellent house reef, Magoodhoo provides exceptional opportunities for snorkeling, swimming, and marine exploration. The island''s welcoming community embraces visitors with traditional Maldivian warmth and shares their pristine way of life with genuine hospitality.","What makes Magoodhoo special is its pristine beauty and unspoiled natural setting. Visitors can enjoy magnificent beaches, explore diverse marine life, and experience traditional island culture while staying in comfortable accommodations that showcase the pristine Maldivian lifestyle."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Magoodhoo Island is strategically located in the northern part of Noonu Atoll, approximately 200 kilometers from Male. This location provides a pristine and magnificent experience while maintaining good accessibility to the capital and international airport through various transfer options.","The island''s position offers excellent protection from strong currents while providing access to pristine coral reefs and diverse marine ecosystems. Its location in the atoll makes it an ideal base for exploring the magnificent beauty and cultural richness of Noonu Atoll."]},{"heading":"Magoodhoo: Pristine Paradise & Marine Magnificence","paragraphs":["Magoodhoo Island is blessed with exceptional natural beauty, featuring pristine white sand beaches, crystal-clear turquoise waters, and one of the most magnificent marine environments in Noonu Atoll. The island offers perfect conditions for beach relaxation and marine exploration in a pristine, unspoiled setting."]},{"heading":"Pristine White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Magoodhoo features magnificent white sand beaches with gentle slopes and calm waters, perfect for swimming, sunbathing, and pristine beach activities."]},{"heading":"Pristine House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef is easily accessible from the beach and offers magnificent snorkeling with diverse marine life and colorful coral formations."]},{"heading":"Pristine Community Life","paragraphs":["Experience the magnificent spirit of traditional island life with friendly locals and pristine Maldivian culture in an unspoiled community setting."]},{"heading":"Pristine Sandbank Adventures","paragraphs":["Explore nearby uninhabited sandbanks and pristine islands around Magoodhoo for magnificent tropical paradise experiences with pristine marine life."]},{"heading":"Beach Features","paragraphs":["Magoodhoo''s coastal highlights include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The waters around Magoodhoo feature:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Magoodhoo offers a perfect blend of marine adventures, cultural experiences, and pristine activities. The island''s magnificent environment and unspoiled atmosphere provide ideal conditions for various activities in a pristine, breathtaking setting."]},{"heading":"Pristine House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the magnificent house reef directly from the beach, featuring colorful corals, tropical fish, and excellent underwater visibility in pristine waters."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving Excursions","paragraphs":["Discover nearby magnificent dive sites with professional dive operators offering guided dives and certification courses in pristine marine environments."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping Adventures","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands, pristine sandbanks, and discover the magnificent beauty of Noonu Atoll with pristine exploration opportunities."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Experience authentic Maldivian fishing techniques with local fishermen during sunset and night fishing trips in pristine community settings."]},{"heading":"Pristine Cultural Immersion","paragraphs":["Learn about local traditions, participate in community activities, and experience magnificent Maldivian island life with pristine cultural exchanges."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy magical sunset cruises around the atoll, often combined with dolphin watching and traditional dhoni sailing in magnificent waters."]},{"heading":"Community Life & Culture","paragraphs":["Magoodhoo is home to approximately 1,100 residents who maintain traditional Maldivian ways of life while embracing sustainable tourism. The island''s community is known for their exceptional friendliness, hospitality, and pristine cultural traditions that create a magnificent atmosphere for visitors."]},{"heading":"Island Life","paragraphs":["Daily life on Magoodhoo follows traditional patterns centered around fishing, family, and community activities. The island maintains a pristine atmosphere where visitors can experience authentic Maldivian culture and customs in their most magnificent, unspoiled setting."]},{"heading":"Local Traditions","paragraphs":["The community preserves traditional Maldivian practices including traditional music, dance, handicrafts, and fishing techniques. Visitors can observe and often participate in cultural activities and celebrations in this pristine island environment."]},{"heading":"Sustainable Tourism","paragraphs":["Magoodhoo has embraced sustainable tourism practices that benefit the local community while preserving the island''s natural environment and pristine cultural heritage for future generations to experience and enjoy."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Magoodhoo Island?","answer":"Regular ferry services are also available from Male and nearby islands in Noonu Atoll."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Magoodhoo?","answer":"The best time to visit Magoodhoo is during the dry season from December to April when weather conditions are most favorable for beach activities and water sports. However, the island can be visited year-round with the wet season offering fewer crowds and a more pristine local atmosphere."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Magoodhoo?","answer":"Magoodhoo offers various budget-friendly accommodations including pristine guesthouses and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, and meal packages in a magnificent setting."},{"question":"What makes Magoodhoo''s community special?","answer":"Magoodhoo is known for its pristine and magnificent community atmosphere. The island maintains authentic Maldivian cultural practices while welcoming visitors with warm hospitality. Visitors can experience genuine cultural exchanges and participate in community activities in this pristine setting."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["foddhoo","manadhoo","velidhoo","miladhoo","maafaru","landhoo","kendhikolhudhoo","henbandhoo","kudafaree","lhohi","maalhendhoo"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'magoodhoo';

-- mahibadhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Mahibadhoo Island from Male?","answer":"You can reach Mahibadhoo Island from Male by public ferry, speedboat, or a combination of domestic flight and speedboat. The public ferry operates three times a week (Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday), departing from Male at 9:00 AM and arriving at Mahibadhoo around 2:00 PM. For the quickest option, you can take a domestic flight to Maamigili Airport (about 30 minutes), followed by a 30-minute speedboat transfer to Mahibadhoo. Some guesthouses also offer private transfers for a higher fee."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Mahibadhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Mahibadhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon (December-April), which generally provides the best overall weather conditions with less rainfall, lower humidity, and calmer seas. This period is ideal for snorkeling, diving, and beach activities. The water visibility is excellent during these months, making it perfect for marine life observation. The southwest monsoon (May-November) may have occasional rain showers but still offers many sunny days, fewer tourists, and lower prices. For whale shark sightings in nearby areas, the period from January to April is best. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) year-round, making Mahibadhoo a viable destination throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Mahibadhoo different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Mahibadhoo stands out from other local islands in the Maldives primarily because of its role as the administrative capital of Alifu Dhaalu Atoll. This gives the island a unique dual character that combines traditional Maldivian island life with more developed administrative infrastructure. Visitors can observe government offices, a regional hospital, educational institutions, and a bustling harbor that serves the entire atoll. The island has better infrastructure than many other local islands, including more reliable utilities, better telecommunications, and more developed banking services. Mahibadhoo also serves as an excellent transportation hub for exploring other islands in the atoll. Despite these modern elements, the island maintains its authentic cultural character and natural beauty, offering visitors a more multifaceted experience than purely tourist-focused islands."},{"question":"Are there any dress code requirements on Mahibadhoo Island?","answer":"Yes, as Mahibadhoo is a local inhabited island with a Muslim population, visitors are expected to dress modestly in public areas. Both men and women should cover shoulders and knees when walking around the village, visiting shops, or dining in local restaurants. Swimwear (including bikinis) is only permitted at the designated \"bikini beach\" area on the eastern side of the island. When visiting the mosque or religious sites, women should cover their heads with a scarf. When visiting government offices or administrative buildings, more formal attire is appreciated. These dress code requirements are a sign of respect for local culture and traditions and are generally well-enforced on the island."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while diving or snorkeling around Mahibadhoo?","answer":"Mahibadhoo is located in Alifu Dhaalu Atoll, which is known for its exceptional marine biodiversity. Snorkelers exploring the house reef can expect to see colorful reef fish, blacktip reef sharks, rays, moray eels, and occasionally turtles. For divers, the nearby dive sites offer even more spectacular marine encounters. The atoll is famous for manta ray cleaning stations where these majestic creatures can be observed year-round, with peak sightings from November to April. Whale shark sightings are possible in certain areas, particularly from January to April. The dive sites feature healthy coral formations, dramatic underwater topography, and pelagic species including eagle rays, Napoleon wrasse, and various shark species. The atoll also has several interesting wreck dives. The variety of marine environments, from shallow lagoons to deep channels, provides diverse underwater experiences suitable for all levels of snorkelers and divers."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'mahibadhoo';

-- makunudhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Makunudhoo Island?","answer":"Due to the island''s remote location in the northern atolls, direct public ferry services from Male are limited. Most guesthouses can arrange the complete transfer from Male, including the domestic flight and speedboat connection. When planning your journey, remember that weather conditions can occasionally affect flight schedules in this region, so it''s advisable to allow buffer days in your travel plans. The journey to Makunudhoo is part of the adventure, offering beautiful aerial views of the Maldivian atolls during the flight and a chance to experience the stunning blue waters during the speedboat transfer."},{"question":"What makes Makunudhoo''s fishing traditions unique in the Maldives?","answer":"Makunudhoo''s fishing traditions stand out in the Maldives because the island has preserved comprehensive knowledge of traditional fishing methods that are both sustainable and highly effective. The island is particularly known for its expertise in pole-and-line tuna fishing, a selective method that targets specific species without the bycatch issues associated with other fishing techniques. This method requires exceptional skill in locating tuna schools, attracting them with live bait, and catching them individually with pole and line. Makunudhoo fishermen maintain deep knowledge of tuna behavior, migration patterns, and feeding habits that has been refined over generations. The community has preserved traditional fish processing techniques including methods for drying, smoking, and curing fish that extend shelf life without refrigeration, creating products like \"valhoamas\" (smoked tuna) that are prized throughout the Maldives. What makes Makunudhoo special is the integration of traditional navigational knowledge with fishing expertise, as fishermen use stars, currents, and natural signs to locate productive fishing grounds without modern technology. The island maintains a strong communal approach to fishing, with practices for sharing catches and supporting community members, reflecting the cooperative nature of traditional Maldivian fishing culture. Makunudhoo is also known for its boat handling skills, with fishermen demonstrating remarkable abilities in managing traditional dhonis in various sea conditions. These preserved traditions offer visitors insights into a sophisticated maritime heritage that connects to centuries of Maldivian cultural identity while demonstrating remarkable adaptation to the marine environment."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Makunudhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Makunudhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. This period is ideal for water activities, with excellent visibility for snorkeling and diving. For those interested in experiencing the island''s fishing traditions, this dry season is also excellent as sea conditions are more favorable for fishing trips, allowing visitors to participate in or observe traditional fishing activities. The southwest monsoon (May to November) brings occasional rain showers but also lower prices and fewer tourists. This season can still be good for cultural experiences, though some fishing and water activities may be affected by rougher seas and reduced visibility. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year. Due to Makunudhoo''s northern location, it may experience slightly different weather patterns than southern atolls, with the possibility of brief rain showers even during the dry season. The shoulder months of November and April often offer a good balance of favorable weather and lower visitor numbers."},{"question":"What type of accommodation can I expect on Makunudhoo?","answer":"Accommodation on Makunudhoo is limited and simple, reflecting the island''s authentic character and minimal tourism development. The island offers a small number of family-run guesthouses that provide clean, comfortable rooms with basic amenities including air conditioning, private bathrooms with hot water, and Wi-Fi (though connectivity may be inconsistent). Most accommodations offer full-board packages that include three home-cooked Maldivian meals daily, featuring fresh seafood caught by local fishermen. Some fishing families also offer homestay experiences where guests stay in a room within a family home, providing deeper cultural immersion and the opportunity to observe traditional fishing activities up close. A few properties are located near the beach, offering easy access to the island''s beautiful shores. Luxury amenities are limited, and the focus is on authentic experiences rather than high-end facilities. What these accommodations lack in luxury, they make up for in personal service, cultural insights, and genuine hospitality. Visitors should adjust their expectations accordingly and embrace the opportunity to experience authentic island life rather than expecting resort-style amenities."},{"question":"Can I participate in fishing activities as a visitor to Makunudhoo?","answer":"Yes, visitors to Makunudhoo have excellent opportunities to participate in fishing activities, which is one of the island''s unique attractions. Most guesthouses can arrange for guests to join traditional fishing trips with local fishermen, providing authentic experiences of Maldivian fishing methods. Morning fishing trips allow you to experience pole-and-line tuna fishing, where you can learn the techniques for attracting and catching tuna in a sustainable manner. Night fishing excursions offer the chance to try hand line fishing for reef fish under the stars, a peaceful and often productive experience that connects you to centuries of fishing tradition. Fish processing demonstrations show how catches are prepared, preserved, and cooked using traditional methods, often with opportunities to taste the results. For those with a deeper interest, extended fishing experiences can sometimes be arranged where you can join fishermen for a full day at sea, learning more about navigation, fish finding, and various fishing techniques. Some accommodations offer more immersive experiences where visitors can stay with fishing families, providing opportunities to observe and participate in the daily rhythm of maritime life. These activities provide not just entertainment but educational insights into a seafaring heritage that has shaped Maldivian culture for centuries. The level of participation can be adjusted based on your interest and comfort level, from simple observation to active involvement in fishing activities."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'makunudhoo';

-- male
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Kaafu Atoll, Central Maldives"},{"label":"City Type","value":"Capital City"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 140,000 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"2.0 km long, 1.0 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Airport","value":"10 km (via speedboat)"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Speedboat, ferry, seaplane"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Capital city, cultural landmarks, government center, historical sites"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Cultural exploration, historical tours, urban experiences, shopping"}],"island_overview":["Located in the heart of Kaafu Atoll, Male City stands as the vibrant capital and cultural epicenter of the Maldives. This densely populated island city serves as the political, economic, and cultural hub of the nation, where centuries of history blend seamlessly with modern urban development to create a unique and fascinating destination unlike anywhere else in the archipelago.","What makes Male truly special is its role as the beating heart of Maldivian civilization, where traditional Islamic culture, colonial influences, and contemporary life converge in a compact urban environment. The city offers visitors an authentic glimpse into the real Maldives beyond the resort experience, showcasing the daily life, traditions, and aspirations of the Maldivian people.","Visitors to Male are drawn by its rich historical heritage, cultural landmarks, vibrant markets, diverse dining scene, architectural treasures, and the opportunity to experience authentic urban Maldivian life. The city serves as both a fascinating destination in its own right and the essential gateway to understanding the broader Maldivian culture and society."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Male City occupies a strategic position in the central part of Kaafu Atoll, approximately 10 kilometers from Velana International Airport. As the capital and largest city of the Maldives, Male serves as the central hub for government, commerce, education, and culture, with all major institutions and services concentrated within its compact urban area.","The city''s location provides excellent connectivity to all parts of the Maldives, serving as the primary departure point for domestic flights, ferry services, and speedboat transfers to resorts and other islands. Despite its small size, Male is one of the most densely populated cities in the world, creating a unique urban environment in the middle of the Indian Ocean."]},{"heading":"Male: Capital City & Cultural Heart","paragraphs":["Male City represents the cultural and historical soul of the Maldives, housing the nation''s most important landmarks, institutions, and cultural treasures. The city offers visitors unparalleled insights into Maldivian history, Islamic culture, and the evolution of this unique island nation."]},{"heading":"Historic Landmarks & Architecture","paragraphs":["Explore iconic landmarks including the Grand Friday Mosque, Presidential Palace, National Museum, and colonial-era buildings that showcase the architectural evolution and cultural heritage of the Maldives."]},{"heading":"Cultural & Religious Sites","paragraphs":["Visit important Islamic sites, traditional mosques, cultural centers, and religious landmarks that demonstrate the deep Islamic heritage and spiritual traditions of Maldivian society."]},{"heading":"Bustling Markets & Commerce","paragraphs":["Experience vibrant local markets, fish markets, produce vendors, and commercial districts that showcase the economic heart of the Maldives and authentic local life."]},{"heading":"Urban Maldivian Life","paragraphs":["Witness authentic urban Maldivian culture through street life, local cafes, government buildings, educational institutions, and the daily rhythms of capital city living."]},{"heading":"Major Landmarks","paragraphs":["Male''s most significant cultural and historical sites:"]},{"heading":"Cultural Experiences","paragraphs":["Authentic urban cultural activities and attractions:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Male City offers unique urban cultural experiences that showcase the authentic heart of Maldivian civilization. From historical landmarks and cultural sites to bustling markets and government buildings, the capital provides insights into the real Maldives beyond the resort experience."]},{"heading":"Historical & Cultural Tours","paragraphs":["Explore Male''s rich history through guided tours of landmarks, museums, mosques, and government buildings that tell the story of Maldivian civilization."]},{"heading":"Market & Shopping Experiences","paragraphs":["Immerse yourself in local life through visits to fish markets, produce markets, and shopping districts that showcase authentic Maldivian commerce and culture."]},{"heading":"Religious & Spiritual Sites","paragraphs":["Visit important mosques, Islamic cultural centers, and religious landmarks that demonstrate the deep spiritual heritage of Maldivian society."]},{"heading":"Urban Life & Architecture","paragraphs":["Experience authentic urban Maldivian life through street walks, architectural tours, and observations of daily life in the capital city."]},{"heading":"Day Trips & Excursions","paragraphs":["Use Male as a base for day trips to nearby islands, resorts, and cultural sites throughout Kaafu Atoll and beyond."]},{"heading":"Waterfront & Recreation","paragraphs":["Enjoy the artificial beach, waterfront promenades, and recreational areas that provide relaxation within the urban environment."]},{"heading":"People & Urban Life","paragraphs":["Male City is home to approximately 140,000 residents, making it one of the most densely populated cities in the world. This vibrant urban community represents the heart of Maldivian society, where government officials, business leaders, students, and families from across the archipelago come together to create a dynamic and diverse metropolitan environment."]},{"heading":"Capital City Dynamics","paragraphs":["As the seat of government and center of commerce, Male attracts the most educated and ambitious Maldivians, creating a sophisticated urban culture that balances traditional Islamic values with modern aspirations. The city serves as the intellectual and cultural hub of the nation, where policy decisions are made and cultural trends are established."]},{"heading":"Cultural Melting Pot","paragraphs":["Male''s population includes people from all inhabited islands of the Maldives, creating a unique cultural melting pot where different island traditions and dialects converge. This diversity, combined with international influences from diplomats, business people, and tourists, creates a cosmopolitan atmosphere unlike anywhere else in the Maldives."]},{"heading":"Urban Challenges and Innovations","paragraphs":["The city faces unique challenges related to its extreme population density and limited space, leading to innovative urban solutions and vertical development. Despite these challenges, Male maintains its cultural identity and community spirit, with strong neighborhood bonds and active civic participation in community life."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"What makes Male City special for cultural exploration?","answer":"Male City is the cultural and historical heart of the Maldives, housing the nation''s most important landmarks including the Grand Friday Mosque, Presidential Palace, National Museum, and UNESCO World Heritage sites. As the capital, it offers unparalleled insights into Maldivian history, Islamic culture, government, and authentic urban life. The city provides the most comprehensive cultural experience available in the Maldives, showcasing everything from ancient architecture to modern political institutions."},{"question":"What are the main attractions and landmarks in Male?","answer":"Male''s major attractions include the Grand Friday Mosque with its golden dome, the Presidential Palace, National Museum with historical artifacts, Old Friday Mosque (UNESCO site), Sultan Park, Republic Square, vibrant fish and local markets, Artificial Beach, and various government buildings. The city also offers cultural walking tours, traditional architecture exploration, and authentic market experiences that showcase the real Maldivian way of life beyond the resort experience."},{"question":"How do I get to Male City from the airport?","answer":"The speedboat is the most common and convenient option, operating regularly throughout the day. Most hotels can arrange transfers, and the journey offers beautiful views of the atoll and surrounding islands."},{"question":"What should I know about visiting Male as a tourist?","answer":"When visiting Male, dress conservatively covering shoulders and knees, respect Islamic customs especially during prayer times, and be mindful when photographing people or government buildings. The city is very walkable but traffic can be busy with motorcycles. Male offers authentic cultural experiences, excellent local cuisine, shopping opportunities, and serves as the perfect base for understanding Maldivian society and culture. Most attractions are within walking distance, and guided tours are highly recommended for historical context."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["himmafushi","huraa","dhiffushi","maafushi","gulhi","guraidhoo","hulhumale","kaashidhoo","gaafaru"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'male';

